                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8a419662-46d2-4d4c-b1ef-3ff32375b64a",
                        "source_entry_id": "90b52914-adf1-32fa-8bc6-e2beacc1f031",
                        "source_sense_id": "30418007-a45c-3d38-b761-8850f62b79b9",
                        "target_lang_word": "afterlife",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "afterlife",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                        "entry_id": "765bd96a-80ed-3de0-b7df-14bb2eabc386",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A conscious existence after death; a supernatural life that follows one's natural life, in some worldviews."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "17604b1d-0c7d-4326-8642-ac8a71cc0c8b",
                                                "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                                "text": "Many religious people believe in an afterlife."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0a06b69-b3c2-47e4-9107-91f843410d4e",
                                                "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                                "text": "Read in euery Starre, and let the Moone be your Candle to doe it, the prouident disposition of God, the eternitie of your after-life."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "76eabcd4-45ab-46a3-9e2c-b4d28d789e83",
                                                "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                                "text": "1715, Alexander Pope, The Temple of Fame, London: Bernard Lintott, Note to p. 16, ver. 5,\nThose heroick Barbarians accounted it a Dishonour to die in their Beds, and rush’d on to certain Death in the Prospect of an After-Life […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ae14316a-bdb6-49c9-b409-52fe80fd9968",
                                                "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                                "text": "1891, Ambrose Bierce, “A Watcher by the Dead” in Tales of Soldiers and Civilians, San Francisco: E.L.G. Steele, p. 175,\nI, who have not a shade of superstition in my nature—I, who have no belief in immortality—I, who know […] that the after-life is the dream of a desire"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "82519662-4879-45dc-a844-752531e49818",
                                                "sense_id": "badf3b60-4371-37c9-bc0f-0f7f04766e74",
                                                "text": "Each person took this paper money and dropped it into a huge tin drum which held within it a fierce fire, a bonfire of heavenly money for Tiger’s afterlife."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2050328c-c08e-3b3e-825c-7753f59a0e46",
                                        "entry_id": "765bd96a-80ed-3de0-b7df-14bb2eabc386",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The place believed to be inhabited by people who have died."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3b951271-0fba-45fa-a5c9-56ea630db1f7",
                                                "sense_id": "2050328c-c08e-3b3e-825c-7753f59a0e46",
                                                "text": "You'll reunite later, somewhere in the afterlife."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a758c3b0-6033-4dcc-abc4-8b85881ba232",
                                                "sense_id": "2050328c-c08e-3b3e-825c-7753f59a0e46",
                                                "text": "There were human cultures that taught an afterlife of the blessed on mountaintops or in clouds, in caverns or oases, but she could not recall any in which if you were very, very good when you died you went to the beach."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1d038bb1-a348-4d74-9664-81f5fedb42e8",
                                                "sense_id": "2050328c-c08e-3b3e-825c-7753f59a0e46",
                                                "text": "He wanted to offer a little reminder that the world is cruel and pointless, all human endeavour ultimately meaningless, and no advancement in this world worth making besides gaining God’s favour and an entry ticket into the better half of the afterlife."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                        "entry_id": "765bd96a-80ed-3de0-b7df-14bb2eabc386",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The part of a person's life that follows a particular stage or event; later life."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "informal",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cd26471e-6074-4029-ad7e-627c2e163647",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "They say that life begins at 40, but right now I'm more interested in the afterlife that begins at 65!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8becec26-b569-4fd8-88d2-fd61a2516267",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "Those Favorites as it is their first care, to hold up themselves in that height of grace, so alwayes make it their second endeavour to raise Estates, to get Offices and governments, that if they doe remove from that height of favour, yet they may still retaine some happy monument of their former power, and a stay to their after-life."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e37ce643-3fd9-458b-8f2b-b05a6b0c0d3d",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "In early youth, not only love, but friendship, at first sight, grows out of an ill-directed sensibility; and in after-life, women under the powerful influence of this temper […] are too readily inclined to select for their confidential connections, flexible and flattering companions […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6859be69-d971-43c2-9a26-b3c764a62680",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "And all the colour of my afterlife / Will be the shadow of today."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d1eb6812-57e8-4c39-ad0c-1c31dae3b3a3",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "[…] the sorrows of childhood, like the pleasures of after life, are transient."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e9fea87e-ce7d-4bf1-a798-a504d94b554b",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "These trembling children have at least this consolation—that never, in their after lives, no, not when poison-gas blinds and chokes them, not when their wives betray them, not when men scorn them and spit upon them, will they know again such terror, despair and loneliness as in the happy carefree schooldays of their youth."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7dd27eb3-b290-42d2-8e1e-b0cab7f3007e",
                                                "sense_id": "f4bbda74-be42-3c9c-a440-92ddd3e91807",
                                                "text": "Mary’s body was a battleground from the beginning. Who was she before the angel called? Why was she chosen, why not some other good girl? Did she suffer pain in childbirth? What was her afterlife? Did she have more children?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a29a0914-3ab8-3e42-b4d4-57aa4857eb42",
                                        "entry_id": "765bd96a-80ed-3de0-b7df-14bb2eabc386",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The effects of a person's actions, or their reputation, after death."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c76d6424-07fa-4220-8181-edebe7e6fdf3",
                                                "sense_id": "a29a0914-3ab8-3e42-b4d4-57aa4857eb42",
                                                "text": "The philanthropic endowment that she bequeathed gave her an ongoing afterlife."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7b834f1a-1e53-406f-9a3a-5d64b9d077f5",
                                                "sense_id": "a29a0914-3ab8-3e42-b4d4-57aa4857eb42",
                                                "text": "1662, Margaret Cavendish, The Several Wits, Scene 34, in Playes, London: John Martyn et al., p. 111,\n[…] poor poverty and birth, can be no hindrance to natural wit, for natural wit, in a poor Cottage, may spin an after-life, enter-weaving several colour’d fancies, and threeds of opinions, making fine and curious Tapestries to hang in the Chambers of fame,"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "23695f97-3481-4c00-92a5-19886db94d2e",
                                                "sense_id": "a29a0914-3ab8-3e42-b4d4-57aa4857eb42",
                                                "text": "Let every Man who votes consider, That he is now going to give away that, for which the Soldier gave up his Rest, his Pleasure, and his Life; the Scholar resign’d his whole Series of Thought, his Midnight Repose, and his Morning Slumbers. In a Word, he is (as I may say) to be Judge of that After-Life, which noble Spirits prefer to their very real Beings."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b7261a32-9ac1-3e00-8446-a80c7fcf7c3c",
                                        "entry_id": "765bd96a-80ed-3de0-b7df-14bb2eabc386",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The events or situations that result from a particular event; the later reception, consumption or reworking of something, especially a cultural production such as a film, book, etc."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c2c83a45-e3db-483a-97f9-44a4da0d0ed7",
                                                "sense_id": "b7261a32-9ac1-3e00-8446-a80c7fcf7c3c",
                                                "text": "The 1970s TV show M*A*S*H had a long afterlife in syndication."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a52b0949-3f53-4e62-a418-95860e688f27",
                                                "sense_id": "b7261a32-9ac1-3e00-8446-a80c7fcf7c3c",
                                                "text": "1969, Harry Zohn (translator), “The Task of the Translator” in Illuminations by Walter Benjamin, New York: Schocken Books, p. 71,\nThe history of the great works of art tells us about their antecedents, their realization in the age of the artist, their potentially eternal afterlife in succeeding generations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00f75a05-5766-4d1d-a0be-aec58d652cac",
                                                "sense_id": "b7261a32-9ac1-3e00-8446-a80c7fcf7c3c",
                                                "text": "Europe in the Twenties and especially the Thirties entered a twilight zone between the afterlife of one war and the looming anticipation of another."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "59ac3ff3-671d-44c8-9cdd-666dafef0e69",
                                                "sense_id": "b7261a32-9ac1-3e00-8446-a80c7fcf7c3c",
                                                "text": "Even in its eighties habitat, Phil Collins’s signature style was rarely mistaken for hip, but his hits have definitely taken on a robust afterlife."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
